{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/932512-2e-houserules-for-pledges","id":932512,"title":"[2e] Houserules for Pledges","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-07-16T08:01:48-07:00","id":932512,"title":"[2e] Houserules for Pledges","content":"Some people don't like how Pledges lost some of their bite in 2e. These houserules are meant to address those issues while mainting the advantages of the new system, particularly how streamlined it is.<br /> <br /> <u><b>General</b></u><br /> * There is no effective limit to the number of Sealings, Oaths, and Bargains a Changeling may make.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Sealings</b></u><br /> * Once a successful seal is made, select a Minor Sanction from the list below. If hey wish to strengthen the Sealing, they may spend a point of willpower to instead apply a Medial Sanction or a willpower dot to apply a Major Sanction.<br /> * If the fae wishes, they may also apply a Boon to the sealing. A Boon is a benefit that applies if the subject succeeds in their statement.<br /> * Mortals can receive a boon of a value up to the sanction on the sealing. Other supernatural creatures may receive a boon of up to a value one less than the sanction (so a Major Sanction allows for a Medial or Minor Boon, a Medial Sanction allows for a Minor Boon, and a Minor Sanction doesn't allow for a boon). Fae creatures may not receive Boons from Sealings, but see Bargains (pg. XX) and Oaths.<br /> * Boons and from multiple Sealings do not stack.<br /> * Only one Luck Sanction and one relevant Ineptitude Sanction may apply to a roll. Use the most powerful currently afflicting someone..<br /> * The same statement can not be sealed multiple times.<br /> <br /> <b>Minor Sanctions.</b><br /> <i>Luck:</i> -1 to all rolls for a month.<br /> <i>Ineptitude:</i> -2 to all rolls based on a certain attribute or skill for a month.<br /> <i>Curse:</i> Removes a one-dot merit, or lowers its value by one for a month.<br /> <i>Flaw:</i> A minor supernatural effect (character\u2019s reflection faces the wrong way, character causes milk to spoil, cats scratch or bite the character, etc.)<br /> <br /> <b>Medial Sanctions</b><br /> <i>Luck:</i> -2 to all rolls for a month.<br /> <i>Ineptitude:</i> -3 to all rolls based on a certain attribute or skill for a month.<br /> <i>Curse:</i> Removes a two dots worth of merits, split how the changeling sees fit, for a month.<br /> <i>Pishogue:</i> The fae may use one of their Contracts and set that as the sanction. Pay the contract's activation cost in addition to the cost for the Sealing. Roll the contract's activation roll and note the number of successes. The contract takes effect with that number of successes when the Sealing is broken.<br /> <i>Flaw: </i>The character gains an appropriate condition either chosen by the Wyrd or chosen by the fae with storyteller approval. If the fae conveys the condition to the other person when doing the sealing, they can also communicate a different resolution that replaces the condition's normal resolution. A beautiful actress is cursed with a disfigurement until she gets someone to love her in her new appearance. Someone who takes more from their neighbor's garden is struck mad until they give back what they stole seven fold. The resolution can not be based on something the fae has control over (&quot;until I forgive them!&quot;), it can't be subjective (&quot;until he learns his lesson!&quot;), nor can it be suicidal (&quot;until they get hit by a bus!&quot;), The Wyrd prizes dramatically ironic resolutions that create interesting narratives and won't honor resolutions that create stagnation. Persistent conditions may not be selected.<br /> <br /> <b>Major Sanctions</b><br /> <i>Luck:</i> -3 to all rolls for a year and a day.<br /> <i>Ineptitude:</i> -5 to all rolls based on a certain attribute or skill for a year and a day.<br /> <i>Curse:</i> Removes a five dots worth of merits, split how the changeling sees fit, for a year and a day.<br /> <i>Death:</i> The target dies in some dramatically ironic fashion one week from when they break the sealing. If the fae who made this sealing forgives the mortal in that time, then the Sanction is averted and the fae recovers the willpower dot they expended. In order to use this sanction, the person must have explicity placed their life on the line when making their statement of intent.<br /> <i>Flaw:</i> As a medial flaw, except Persistent conditions may be selected. Instead of changing the condition's resolution, the fae may add something that must be done before the condition may be resolved normally. This extra task is often arduous, or even seemingly impossible.<br /> <br /> <b>Minor Boons</b><br /> <i>Luck:</i> +1 to all rolls for a week.<br /> <i>Adroitness:</i> +2 to all rolls based on a certain attribute or skill for a week.<br /> <i>Skill:</i> +3 to all rolls based on a certain Skill for a week.<br /> <i>Blessing:</i> Grants one dot worth of merits, whether a new merit or increasing an existing merit, for a month.<br /> <br /> <b>\u200bMedial Boons</b><br /> <i>Luck:</i> +2 to all rolls for a week.<br /> <i>Adroitness:</i> +3 to all rolls based on a certain attribute or skill for a week.<br /> <i>Blessing:</i> Grants two dots worth of merits, whether in new merits or increasing existing merits, for a month.<br /> <br /> <b>Major Boon</b><br /> <i>Luck:</i> +3 to all rolls for a month.<br /> <i>Adroitness:</i> +5 to all rolls based on a certain attribute or skill for a week.<br /> <i>Blessing:</i> Grants five dots worth of merits, whether in new merits or increasing existing merits, for a month.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Oaths</u></b><br /> * Not sure what to do with these just yet.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Bargains</b></u><br /> * The Changeling must show the mortal some aspect of their magic, not necessarily their true form. Any obvious display will do.<br /> * Bargains may not be supernaturally coerced.<br /> * A mortal who breaks a Bargain suffers the Delusional Condition and a Medial Sanction, as though they had broken a Sealing. It does not automatically mean a Huntsmen gets them.<br /> * The fae also loses their advantages if the mortal breaks their part in the Bargain.<br /> * Supernatural creatures having odd effects on a Bargain is an optional rule.<br /> * A Changeling's end of the Bargain may not be fulfilled by a Boon from a Sealing or an Oaths.<br /> * A fae may select one of the following benefits when sealing a Bargain:<br /> - Hiding: Apply a cumulative -1 penalty to all hunting or investigation rolls to find her, whether from a Huntsmen or otherwise.<br /> - Glamour: Gain 1 Glamour per day. This requires that the mortal's task be some sort of offering made at least once per week.<br /> - Ensorcellment: The mortal can see through the mask as though they were a Changeling, an they may use the Changeling's Wyrd in place of their own Supernatural Potency when contesting the powers of fae creatures.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Changelog</b></u><br /> <i>7/18/16 </i><br /> - Combined Talent/Skill split into Ineptitude (Sanctions) and Adroitness (Boons)<br /> - The Luck and Ineptitude Sanctions no longer stack with themselves, but they do stack with each other.<br /> - Changed the Major Blessing/Curse to 5 merit dots.<br /> - The Death sanction now requires the hapless person to bet their life or otherwise imply it is on the line in order for that sanction to be chosen.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1157,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1157-soulgambit","user":"SoulGambit","date":". 07-18-2016, 12:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1157-soulgambit","name":"SoulGambit","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"912"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}